95000},"score":40.228447,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/opg.optica.org\/abstract.cfm?URI=optica-2-11-933"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2015,10,29]]},"references-count":29,"journal-issue":{"issue":"11","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2015]]},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2015]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1364\/optica.2.000933","ISSN":["2334-2536"],"issn-type":[{"value":"2334-2536","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2015,10,29]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,24]],"date-time":"2024-12-24T17:40:33Z","timestamp":1735062033785,"version":"3.32.0"},"publisher-location":"Washington, D.C.","reference-count":4,"publisher":"Optica Publishing Group","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>High-speed and high-precision optical focus control is crucial for optical systems like 3D laser-scanning microscopy and laser micromachining. Typically, the 3D focus control requires a 2D beam scanner such as galvanometric scanners for lateral scanning and a piezo actuator for axial scanning [1]. Another route for the focus control involves wavefront shaping using spatial light modulator (SLM) such as liquid-crystal-based SLM and digital micromirror device (DMD) [2]. This technique allows the random-access scanning of focal spots, making it useful for laser-scanning microscopy and high-throughput laser micromachining. Unfortunately, its focus control speed is inherently restricted by the SLM\u2019s refresh rate, which is around several tens of kHz.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1364\/jsapo.2024.16p_a37_5","type":"proceedings-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,24]],"date-time":"2024-12-24T17:13:04Z","timestamp":1735060384000},"page":"16p_A37_5","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Ultra-wide field-of-view optical focusing with high-speed complex wavefront shaping"],"prefix":"10.1364","author":[{"given":"Atsushi","family":"Shibukawa","sequence":"first","af